\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{Two independent link weights give a convolution}
\date{2026-11-11}

\p{Take a path with two edges in traversal order, carrying
#{U_1,U_2}, and retain #{u=U_2U_1}. Let #{G} be compact
metrizable and let #{k_1,k_2} be nonnegative integrable
densities of mass one against Haar probability. For the
fine weight #{w(U_1,U_2)=k_1(U_1)k_2(U_2)}, the
[effective gauge weight](fdip-00MX), without imposing
vertex gauge invariance, is
##{\rho=\mu_G,\qquad
 f(u)=\int_G k_2(ux^{-1})k_1(x)\,\extd\mu_G(x)
     =(k_2*k_1)(u).}
Indeed the Haar-preserving change
#{(U_1,U_2)\mapsto(x,u)=(U_1,U_2U_1)}
is a coordinate replacement of \ref{fdip-00MR}, and its
inverse gives this density through \ref{fdip-00MQ}.
The mass is one by Tonelli.}

\p{These individual link weights need not be invariant
under independent endpoint frame changes. For central
weights the same convolution is the shared-edge
contraction of two face weights in
\ref{fdip-007C}, with the remaining boundary factors
treated as fixed. Choosing both densities to be the
SU(2) Wilson weight makes the family comparison of
\ref{fdip-00MZ} a calculation of this exact retained
function.}
